    assert r.lin > 0,      "Error: Not a positive height"
    # an assertion that width of r is positive
    assert r.exp > 0,      "Error: Not a positive width"
    # an assertion that width x height of r is equal to mat
    assert r.exp*r.lin == r.mat, "Error: dimension does not conform to matric value"
    return r

This way, you would create a rectangle with a width, height, and matric property. Then you can validate these properties within your function. Any violations of these conditions will cause an AssertionError with a corresponding message.
This is a very simple example and there are much more sophisticated ways to handle potential errors in Python. For instance, you could return None (or some other indicator of failure) and then handle the potential failure later in your code. But in simple cases such as this one, assertions can be very helpful.
For more details about assertions in general, check out the Python documentation. 
https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/errors.html
For more into how assertions can be used in your situation, check out a related StackOverflow question.
https://stackoverflow.com/questions/3959492/python-assert-usefulness-and-best-practices
Q: Is it a good practice to remove a user from a table after they are deleted from another table? Assuming we're working with a relational database with 2 tables, User and Order. Each Order table can have a corresponding User table, but each User can have multiple Order entries. 
In practical terms, what's the best practice to remove a User from the User table once that user is deleted from the Order table? 
My current thought is to create a foreign key relationship between User and Order tables and delete the related entries in User table whenever an entry in Order table gets deleted but I was wondering if there are any best practices or commonly used methodologies to handle this situation. 

A: It is a good practice to track deletions, so you can maintain history and integrity. Any time an entry from Order table is deleted, you should mark the related User as deleted as well or perform a soft delete.
In terms of best practices, it is quite common to use cascade delete in relation to the foreign key constraint to maintain data integrity. In case of a User being deleted, it automatically makes all associated Orders deleted as well.
So yes, keep track of deleted users and you can maintain history of User and Order data.
But remember, it makes sense to keep data even if user is deleted, for example, if there may need to be historical data analysis. If a lot of such data are kept, memory issue might occur. But again, you need to decide based on the specific use case.

A: Typically, in relational databases you would maintain historical data to keep the integrity and traceability of all previous transactions made with a user. If a user is deleted (maybe because they have left the company or been fired), you would, probably, keep their user details unchanged, disable their access or make a record of the event on a separate "Deleted Users" table. 
The Orders would still be connected to the Deleted User with a foreign key, depending on your application requirements. 
That's often referred to as a soft delete. 
Note that it's not a technical issue with SQL, it's more about database design and maintenance strategy.

A: It's a good practice to keep track of user activity in all the tables in order to maintain the integrity of your historical data.
In this scenario, you don't need to delete users from the User table, instead, you can keep a flag or a column in the User table to denote a deleted user. This column will be defaulted to some value like 'Inactive' or 1, and whenever the user order is deleted or not you can set this column value to 'Deleted' or 0.
This way when a user is deleted from the Order table, you would just mark the user as Inactive in the User table. This way you can maintain a history of users, even though they may not have any orders anymore in the Order table. 
This method also keeps the data intact, and you can easily query your data based on user status (active/deleted). 
Over time, if any user is active and has made a number of orders then it may consume more storage space. But at the same time, it allows you to maintain the history of all users.

A: While it is a good practice to keep a history of a deleted users (like moving them to an archive table), it depends on your requirements. 
